# Optional password authentication

## Scope

The existing global `User` identity may have one optional password credential.
Workspace membership, tenant status, operator-role restrictions and the separate
platform-operations identity remain independent authorization checks. A newly
registered user is verified through the existing OTP flow and is never required to
set a password during registration. Portal remains the authority for the member
session cookie, as specified in `member-session-portal-authority.mdoc`.

## Credential and session requirements

Store only a salted, versioned password hash using a password-specific KDF and
documented work factors. The password itself is never logged, persisted in browser
state, placed in URLs, or returned by an API. Validate passwords consistently on
creation and verification, accept Unicode and spaces, permit long passwords, and
reject common or compromised choices. Verify unknown accounts against a dummy hash
and return a generic authentication failure; apply distributed abuse controls.
The offline blocklist includes the entries of at least 15 characters from the
NCSC 100,000 common-password list pinned in `common-passwords.ts`, along with
service-specific examples and repeated patterns. It is a baseline rather than a
complete breach corpus; maintainers must review and refresh its source periodically.

Creating or changing a password requires an authenticated user's own session and a
fresh single-use OTP challenge bound to that user, current mobile, tenant and the
password-change purpose. A login or mobile-change OTP must not authorize this action.
The credential update, challenge consumption and cross-tenant session-version
invalidation must be atomic. Reissue the current session with the new version. A
read-only support session cannot create or change credentials. Never accept a target
user identifier from the browser for a self-service credential operation.

Password login must reapply the same tenant, active membership, role, suspended
tenant and host checks as OTP login; it must not complete new-user onboarding.
For portal and marketing, the portal BFF writes the member cookie. The operator BFF
retains its existing owner-panel check. Password login does not change platform-ops
authentication or confer additional workspace permissions.

`PASSWORD_AUTH_ENABLED` defaults off. Password login additionally requires Redis
and `PASSWORD_AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_SECRET` containing 32 random bytes encoded as 64 hex
characters. The secret is configured server-side, never in client environment,
source control, logs or test evidence. Redis failures must stop password login;
they must not bypass the attempt budget. The caller must supply a verified client
IP obtained through a trusted proxy chain; deployment must prove this value is not
an attacker-supplied header or a shared CDN address before enabling login.
`PASSWORD_AUTH_INGRESS_SECRET` is a separate 32-byte hex secret shared only by the
API and the portal/operator BFFs; it authenticates the short-lived client-IP proof
bound to the tenant and phone. Never expose this proof or secret to the browser.

Portal and operator BFFs require `PASSWORD_AUTH_CLIENT_IP_MODE` in production:
`direct` accepts only the `X-Real-IP` that the private ingress overwrites with
its network peer; `trusted-edge` additionally requires comma-separated
`PASSWORD_AUTH_TRUSTED_EDGE_CIDRS` and accepts `Ar-Real-IP` only when that peer
matches one of the configured ranges. The latter rejects direct traffic rather
than trusting its supplied header. For Arvan, obtain the current ranges from its
official published list and maintain them as they change. Before enabling this
mode, verify that the CDN overwrites a client-supplied `Ar-Real-IP`, that direct
requests cannot forge `X-Real-IP`, and that application ports are private. These
requirements apply separately to staging and production. A missing or malformed
mode, range, or client IP disables password login rather than bypassing limits.

## Verification

Before rollout, cover password hashing, policy, generic failures, request throttling,
OTP purpose/identity/tenant binding, replay, expiry, concurrency, session revocation,
cross-tenant isolation and read-only support. Exercise OTP registration and both
login methods on portal, marketing modal and operator surfaces in a real browser.
Confirm staged migration compatibility, deployment revision and staging browser
results separately from local and CI evidence.
